# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Raw upstream payloads MUST NEVER enter this tree.
# Constitution Principle V: "'Published' covers everything a third party can
# reach: ... and any fixture, sample, or test file in the repository."
# This list is the passive half of that guard; tools/guard_no_raw_payloads.py
# is the active half. Neither is sufficient alone.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
spike/out/
data/raw/
*.payload.json
*.raw.json
*.raw.csv

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Published dataset -- BUILD OUTPUT, not source (owner decision 2026-10-09).
#
# `make refresh` writes data/published/ locally. It is NOT committed on `main`:
# the spike measured 216.5 MiB per refresh (227,007,149 bytes, both formats,
# full window), and because T008 concluded the refresh must commit on every run
# to keep GitHub's 60-day schedule rule from silently disabling it, committing
# the dataset to `main` would breach GitHub's "ideally less than 1 GB"
# repository guidance on about the FIFTH refresh.
#
# The dataset lives only on the rolling orphan branch `published`, which the
# workflow force-pushes as a single commit. That bounds repository growth; its
# cost is the per-refresh provenance that git history would otherwise give.
#
# Deliberately NO .gitkeep under data/published/ -- a committed .gitkeep is
# exactly how a git-ignored build-output directory ends up tracked on `main`.
# data/assertions/ DOES keep its .gitkeep: maintainer corrections are an input
# that must survive a refresh rewriting the published branch wholesale.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
data/published/

# Scratch space for fetched bodies lives OUTSIDE the repo ($SANSAD_SCRATCH).
# This entry exists only to catch a scratch dir created inside the tree by
# mistake; `make scratch` asserts against that case.
sansad-scratch/

